    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Penn-State-University-Park/Pennsylvania-Legislative-Science---Technology-Policy-Fellow--Joint-State-Government-Commission-_REQ_0000080912-2": {
      "title": "Pennsylvania Legislative Science & Technology Policy Fellow (Joint State Government Commission)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Commonwealth of Pennsylvania Science and Technology Policy (COPA-STEP) Program COPA-STEP, part of the Law, Policy and Engineering (LPE) Initiative in the College of Engineering, serves as the applied practice branch of LPE’s interdisciplinary mission. While LPE drives interdisciplinary education in law, policy, and engineering, COPA-STEP specializes in real-world policy engagement and professional development, reinforcing LPE's mission to produce actionable knowledge that supports public, environmental, and scientific welfare. COPA-STEP aims to strengthen ties between the scientific community and the General Assembly in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, while encouraging and equipping more scientists and engineers with the skills for effective careers in government and public service. 2026-2027 Pennsylvania Legislative Science and Technology Policy Fellowship The Pennsylvania General Assembly is the largest full-time legislature in the nation and regularly deals with science or technology-related issues. Yet, legislators and staff, as well as advisory bodies could utilize science, technology, engineering and math (STEM) expertise to keep up with increasing science and technology issues facing the Commonwealth. Pennsylvania Science and Technology Policy Fellows help bridge this gap to provide science and technical expertise to lawmakers. Thus, COPA-STEP is recruiting for a one-year Legislative Science & Technology Policy Fellow embedded in the Joint State Government Commission. The Joint State Government Commission (JSGC) serves as a bipartisan and bicameral research agency of the PA General Assembly. The JSGC provides the legislature with a readily available mechanism for conducting interdisciplinary studies. The nonpartisan Fellow will embed with the staff of JSGC in Harrisburg, PA, for twelve months starting Fall 2026. The Fellow will equip JSGC with the expert knowledge and advanced research, analysis, and writing skills needed to meaningfully contribute to policy work within JSGC and enable the Pennsylvania General Assembly to make sound, data-driven policy and decisions. To ensure a successful fellowship year, the Fellow will receive training from LPE/COPA-STEP in Pennsylvania state government, the Pennsylvania policy process, policy writing instruction, communicating science and technology to non-scientific audiences, and on how to be an “honest broker”, i.e., providing evidence-based, objective, and nonpartisan advice that helps expand or clarify the choices for decision-making in an unbiased way. JSGC staff will manage the day-to-day work of the fellow. The primary job functions and responsibilities of the Fellow are: Conduct and synthesize research, write and edit reports, and create policy recommendations on AI or related topics as assigned by the JSGC. Meet both in-person and virtually with experts and stakeholders who have experience in the topic areas being studied. Assist in preparing meeting materials, including presentations, and attend meetings, seminars, conferences, and other events at the direction of the JSGC. Manage schedule and workflow to ensure reports are completed in a timely manner. Work in team settings. Perform other office and administrative functions as needed.  Attend trainings and weekly or bi-weekly check-ins with LPE/COPA-STEP and provide feedback, as requested. Support occasional LPE/COPA-STEP programming. Eligible candidates must have a Ph.D. or terminal degree in a relevant field (e.g., M.D., D.V.M., M.P.H., M.Eng., or equivalent). Preferred candidates will have strong quantitative empirical research and data visualization skills. In addition, preferred candidates will have exceptional writing skills, the ability to communicate with nontechnical audiences and translate complex ideas into easily digestible information, take initiative and manage their time well. This position is based in Harrisburg, PA and will work in-office at JSGC for the first six months of employment before being allowed to participate in a hybrid in-office/telework schedule. To apply, candidates should provide 1) a cover letter summarizing their background, relevant skills, interests, and goals, interest in the position, and their contact information; 2) a current resume; and 3) names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional wellbeing. In addition to comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage, employees enjoy robust retirement plans. One of the standout benefits is the generous 75% tuition discount, available to employees as well as eligible spouses and children.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Penn-State-University-Park/Pennsylvania-Legislative-Science---Technology-Policy-Fellow--Local-Government-Commission-_REQ_0000080911-2": {
      "title": "Pennsylvania Legislative Science & Technology Policy Fellow (Local Government Commission)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Commonwealth of Pennsylvania Science and Technology Policy (COPA-STEP) Program COPA-STEP, part of the Law, Policy and Engineering (LPE) Initiative in the College of Engineering, serves as the applied practice branch of LPE’s interdisciplinary mission. While LPE drives interdisciplinary education in law, policy, and engineering, COPA-STEP specializes in real-world policy engagement and professional development, reinforcing LPE's mission to produce actionable knowledge that supports public, environmental, and scientific welfare. COPA-STEP aims to strengthen ties between the scientific community and the General Assembly in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, while encouraging and equipping more scientists and engineers with the skills for effective careers in government and public service. 2026-2027 Pennsylvania Legislative Science and Technology Policy Fellowship The Pennsylvania General Assembly is the largest full-time legislature in the nation and regularly deals with science or technology-related issues. Yet, legislators and staff, as well as advisory bodies could utilize science, technology, engineering and math (STEM) expertise to keep up with increasing science and technology issues facing the commonwealth. Pennsylvania Science and Technology Policy Fellows help bridge this gap to provide science and technical expertise to lawmakers. Thus, COPA-STEP is recruiting for a one-year Legislative Science & Technology Policy Fellow embedded in the Local Government Commission. The Local Government Commission (LGC) serves as a bipartisan and bicameral service agency of the PA General Assembly. The LGC provides the legislature with research and resources for more effective and efficient local government. The nonpartisan Fellow will embed with the staff of LGC in Harrisburg, PA, for twelve months starting Fall 2026. The Fellow will equip LGC with the expert knowledge and advanced research, analysis, and writing skills needed to meaningfully contribute to policy work within LGC and enable the Pennsylvania General Assembly to make sound, data-driven policy and decisions affecting local government in the commonwealth. To ensure a successful fellowship year, the Fellow will receive training from LPE/COPA-STEP in Pennsylvania state government, the Pennsylvania policy process, policy writing instruction, communicating science and technology to non-scientific audiences, and on how to be an “honest broker”, i.e., providing evidence-based, objective, and nonpartisan advice that helps expand or clarify the choices for decision-making in an unbiased way. LGC staff will manage the day-to-day work of the fellow. The primary job functions and responsibilities of the Fellow are: Collaborate with the Pennsylvania State Geospatial Coordinating Board, the Pennsylvania Civic Boundary Working Group, LGC staff, and other stakeholders in the analysis and improvement of the methodology for the ascertainment of legal Pennsylvania civic boundary information and accurate representation in geospatial civic boundary data. Conduct and synthesize research, write and edit a civic boundary report including recommendations for legislative or regulatory changes at the state, county, and local level. Assist with the preparation of other reports, and create policy recommendations as assigned by LGC. Meet both in-person and virtually with experts and stakeholders who have experience in the topic areas being studied. Assist in preparing meeting materials, including presentations, and attend meetings, seminars, conferences, and other events at the direction of the LGC. Manage schedule and workflow to ensure reports are completed in a timely manner. Work in team settings. Perform other office and administrative functions, including constituent work and the development of legislative proposals with a heavy emphasis on municipal law, as needed.  Attend trainings and weekly or bi-weekly check-ins with LPE/COPA-STEP and provide feedback, as requested. Support occasional LPE/COPA-STEP programming. Eligible candidates must have a Ph.D. or terminal degree in a relevant field (e.g., M.D., D.V.M., M.P.H., M.Eng., or equivalent). Preferred candidates will have strong quantitative empirical research and data visualization skills. In addition, preferred candidates will have exceptional writing skills, the ability to communicate with nontechnical audiences and translate complex ideas into easily digestible information, take initiative and manage their time well. Strong candidates will demonstrate an interest in surveying, geography, geographic information systems, history, and archival and comparative state law research. This position is based in Harrisburg, PA and will work in-office at LGC for the first two months of employment before being allowed to participate in a hybrid in-office/telework schedule. To apply, candidates should provide 1) a cover letter summarizing their background, relevant skills, interests, and goals, interest in the position, and their contact information; 2) a current resume; and 3) names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional wellbeing. In addition to comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage, employees enjoy robust retirement plans. One of the standout benefits is the generous 75% tuition discount, available to employees as well as eligible spouses and children.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Penn-State-University-Park/Pennsylvania-Legislative-Science---Technology-Policy-Fellow--Center-for-Rural-Pennsylvania-_REQ_0000080907-2": {
      "title": "Pennsylvania Legislative Science & Technology Policy Fellow (Center for Rural Pennsylvania)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Commonwealth of Pennsylvania Science and Technology Policy (COPA-STEP) Program COPA-STEP, part of the Law, Policy and Engineering (LPE) Initiative in the College of Engineering, serves as the applied practice branch of LPE’s interdisciplinary mission. While LPE drives interdisciplinary education in law, policy, and engineering, COPA-STEP specializes in real-world policy engagement and professional development, reinforcing LPE's mission to produce actionable knowledge that supports public, environmental, and scientific welfare. COPA-STEP aims to strengthen ties between the scientific community and the General Assembly in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, while encouraging and equipping more scientists and engineers with the skills for effective careers in government and public service. 2026-2027 Pennsylvania Legislative Science and Technology Policy Fellowship The Pennsylvania General Assembly is the largest full-time legislature in the nation and regularly deals with science or technology-related issues. Yet, legislators and staff, as well as advisory bodies could utilize science, technology, engineering and math (STEM) expertise to keep up with increasing science and technology issues facing the Commonwealth. Pennsylvania Science and Technology Policy Fellows help bridge this gap to provide science and technical expertise to lawmakers. Thus, COPA-STEP is recruiting for a one-year Legislative Science & Technology Policy Fellow embedded in the Center for Rural Pennsylvania. The Center for Rural Pennsylvania (CRP) is a bipartisan, bicameral legislative agency that serves as a resource for rural policy within the Pennsylvania General Assembly. CRP’s work affects approximately 3.4 million residents who live in Pennsylvania’s rural communities. The nonpartisan Fellow will embed with the staff of CRP in Harrisburg, PA, for twelve months starting Fall 2026. The Fellow will equip CRP with the expert knowledge and advanced research, analysis, and writing skills needed to meaningfully contribute to research-grounded policy work within the center and enable the Pennsylvania General Assembly to make sound, data-driven policy and decisions. The agency has set three research themes for the 2026-2027 year including health and wellness, economic and workforce development, and agriculture and the environment. It is anticipated that the Fellow would contribute to one or more of these areas. To ensure a successful fellowship year, the Fellow will receive training from LPE/COPA-STEP in Pennsylvania state government, the Pennsylvania policy process, applied research and policy writing instruction, communicating science and technology to non-scientific audiences, and on how to be an “honest broker”, i.e., providing evidence-based, objective, and nonpartisan research that helps expand or clarify the choices for decision-making in an unbiased way. CRP staff will manage the day-to-day work of the fellow.  The primary job functions and responsibilities of the Fellow are: Conduct and synthesize research, write and edit reports or fact sheets, and create policy recommendations as assigned by the host office. Meet both in-person and virtually with experts and stakeholders who have experience in the topic areas being studied. Support the review and administration of the CRP grant program, which includes a number of external research projects on a variety of topics. Assist in preparing meeting materials, including presentations. Attend legislative committee meetings, Board of Directors meetings, seminars, conferences, and other events at the direction of the host office. Manage schedule and workflow to ensure reports are completed in a timely manner. Work in team settings. Perform other office and administrative functions as needed.  Attend trainings and weekly or bi-weekly check-ins with LPE/COPA-STEP and provide feedback, as requested. Support occasional LPE/COPA-STEP programming. Eligible candidates must have a Ph.D. or terminal degree in a relevant field (e.g., M.D., D.V.M., M.P.H., M.Eng., or equivalent). Preferred candidates will have strong quantitative empirical research and experience with inferential statistics with proficiency in statistical software such as SPSS, Stata, and R. In addition, preferred candidates will have exceptional data visualization and writing skills, the ability to communicate with nontechnical audiences, and translate complex ideas into easily digestible information particularly around the research themes that have been adopted by the agency. The Fellow is expected to take initiative and manage their time well. This position is based in Harrisburg, PA and participates in CRP’s hybrid work schedule with 3 core days in the office and the flexibility to telework the remaining 2 days. To apply, candidates should provide 1) a cover letter summarizing their background, relevant skills, interests, and goals, interest in the position, and their contact information; 2) a current resume; and 3) names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled.   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional wellbeing. In addition to comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage, employees enjoy robust retirement plans. One of the standout benefits is the generous 75% tuition discount, available to employees as well as eligible spouses and children.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Penn-State-University-Park/Researcher_REQ_0000080670-2": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-13\nPosted: Posted 23 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Hanna lab at The Pennsylvania State University is a new, collaborative research group studying the molecular pathways that detect, limit, and repair damage to lysosomes — organelles that are especially vulnerable to injury and whose dysfunction is increasingly implicated in neurodegenerative disease. We are seeking a highly motivated, dedicated, and enthusiastic Researcher , keen to design, implement, and advance our ongoing research activities. In this Researcher position, you will work closely with the PI and researchers at all career stages to shape and execute the experimental priorities on high-impact projects. Working with a high degree of independence, you will apply cutting-edge cell-biological approaches: from live-cell fluorescence microscopy of organelle dynamics to organelle immunoisolation for proteomic and lipidomic characterization and CRISPR/Cas9 genetic editing of mammalian cells. We are a small, close-knit team where creative thinking and persistence are celebrated, and there is plenty of opportunity to learn, contribute ideas, and grow. Job Responsibilities Lead and manage the lab's mammalian cell culture and genetic-editing projects, designing and implementing experiments independently and in coordination with the PI. Design, execute, and advance live-cell and confocal fluorescence microscopy experiments across multiple ongoing projects, and help oversee microscope use in the lab. Develop, apply, and optimize molecular cloning strategies in support of the lab's research goals. Direct and advance the lab's protein and organelle biochemistry efforts, including characterization for proteomic and lipidomic analyses. Collect, analyze, and interpret data; troubleshoot experimental and technical challenges; and maintain accurate, well-organized records. Help train and mentor trainees in laboratory techniques, biosafety practices, lab policies, equipment use, and proper record keeping. Contribute to and help coordinate co-authored publications, grant proposals, presentations, and summary reports. Support day-to-day lab operations and take on additional projects in support of the lab's goals. Education and Experience Applicants must possess a master's degree, or its equivalent , in cell biology, molecular biology, biochemistry, neuroscience, or a related field. A background in cell and molecular biology or biochemistry, together with strong technical, analytical, and communication skills, is expected, as is the ability to work both independently and as part of a team. Familiarity with several of the following is preferred but not required: mammalian cell culture and genetic editing; molecular cloning and standard molecular biology techniques; fluorescence and confocal microscopy; protein and organelle biochemistry, including western blotting and immunoisolation; and image-analysis software. The interview will be an opportunity to discuss these areas in more detail. Additional Information Applications must be submitted electronically and include a cover letter summarizing relevant experience and interest in the position, a curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information of three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to Michael Hanna at michael.hanna@psu.edu . About the Hanna Lab We are a new lab in the Huck Life Sciences Building at the heart of Penn State's University Park campus in State College, PA. Our goal is to do the best, most impactful science possible — and to have fun while doing it. We are committed to a lab culture of mutual respect, kindness, and inclusion, in which people at every career stage are supported, and where accomplishments large and small are celebrated.   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional wellbeing. In addition to comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage, employees enjoy robust retirement plans. One of the standout benefits is the generous 75% tuition discount, available to employees as well as eligible spouses and children.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Penn-State-University-Park/Director--The-Penn-State-Critical-Materials-Alliance_REQ_0000081293-1": {
      "title": "Director, The Penn State Critical Materials Alliance",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants .   POSITION SPECIFICS The Pennsylvania State University invites applications and nominations for the inaugural Director of the Penn State Critical Materials Alliance ( Penn State CMA) , a strategic university-wide initiative focused on advancing research, workforce development, industry engagement, and innovation across the critical minerals and materials value chain. Penn State possesses the breadth required to address the entire critical minerals and materials lifecycle - the new Director will be instrumental in aggregating and focusing expertise across campus .   The Penn State CMA is a university-wide initiative managed by the Materials Research Institute (MRI) on behalf of Penn State's Office of the Senior Vice President for Research. The Alliance brings together faculty and research infrastructure from across the University, including the Colleges of Earth and Mineral Sciences, Engineering, Eberly College of Science, Smeal College of Business, and Information Sciences and Technology, together with Penn State's Applied Research Laboratory (ARL) and major interdisciplinary institutes including the Institute of Energy and the Environment (IEE), National Security Institute (NSI), and Institute for Computational and Data Sciences (ICDS).   The Director will provide visionary leadership for a growing interdisciplinary effort spanning resource discovery and characterization, extraction and separation, advanced materials processing and manufacturing, circular economy approaches, artificial intelligence and supply-chain analytics, and the societal dimensions of critical minerals and materials development. The successful candidate will hold a tenured faculty appointment at the Professor rank in an academic department aligned with their expertise and will work closely with colleges, interdisciplinary institutes, and research centers across the University. Exceptionally accomplished candidates currently at the Associate Professor rank may be considered when their record supports appointment at the Professor rank.   The Director will be responsible for building and leading a nationally recognized interdisciplinary program that positions Penn State as a leader in securing domestic supply chains for critical minerals and materials essential to energy technologies, advanced manufacturing, national security, and economic competitiveness.   Responsibilities   The Director will be expected to :   • Develop and execute a strategic vision for the Penn State CMA .   • Build and lead interdisciplinary teams spanning multiple colleges, institutes, and campuses.   • Foster a collaborative culture that integrates expertise across exploration, extraction, processing, manufacturing, recycling, AI-enabled systems, economics, policy, and workforce development.   • Expand partnerships with industry, federal agencies, national laboratories, state agencies, and international collaborators.   • Develop and lead large-scale research initiatives and center-scale proposals to federal sponsors including DOE, DoD, USGS, NSF, NIST, and related agencies.   • Establish and grow industry partnerships, consortia, and collaborative research programs with Federal Labs ( e.g. DOE Labs) that accelerate technology translation and workforce development.   • Coordinate strategic investments in shared research infrastructure, pilot-scale testbeds, and workforce programs that support critical minerals and materials innovation.   • Advance educational and workforce initiatives that prepare students, postdoctoral scholars, and technical professionals for careers in the critical minerals and materials ecosystem.   • Serve as an advocate and spokesperson for Penn State's critical minerals and materials activities at the regional, national, and international levels.   • Maintain an internationally recognized program of scholarship consistent with appointment at the rank of Professor.   Director Resources   The successful candidate will receive a competitive salary and startup package commensurate with their experience and consistent with Penn State policies. In addition, Penn State is committed to providing the resources necessary to establish the Penn State Critical Materials Alliance as a nationally recognized center of excellence that convenes academia, industry, and government . This support will include the operational and programmatic infrastructure needed to foster interdisciplinary collaboration, develop strategic partnerships, and accelerate the growth of large-scale research and workforce initiatives. Resources will be provided through a collaborative investment involving the University, participating colleges, and interdisciplinary institutes, with the goal of establishing a sustainable, nationally recognized Alliance.   Required Qualifications   Candidates must possess :   • An earned doctorate in engineering, materials science, geosciences, chemistry, physics, or a closely related field.   • Qualifications commensurate with appointment as a tenured Professor at Penn State ; exceptionally accomplished Associate Professors may be considered when their record supports appointment at the Professor rank.   • An internationally recognized record of scholarship and research accomplishments relevant to critical minerals, materials, energy technologies, advanced manufacturing, resource systems, recycling, supply chains, or related fields.   • A demonstrated record of leading large interdisciplinary research programs, centers, institutes, consortia, or comparable collaborative efforts, with the stature and credibility to lead senior faculty across multiple academic units.   • Strong communication and relationship-building skills with academic, industry, government, and community stakeholders.   • Demonstrated commitment to fostering collaborative and inclusive research environments.   • US Citizen or Green Card Holder     Preferred Qualifications   Preference will be given to candidates with:   • Experience leading multi-institutional or center-scale research initiatives.   • Experience working with federal agencies and national laboratories.   • Experience developing industry partnerships and translational research programs.   • Knowledge of one or more areas relevant to the critical minerals and materials value chain, including exploration, extraction and separation, advanced manufacturing, circular economy and recycling, supply-chain resilience, artificial intelligence and digital systems.   • Experience building workforce development, educational, or training programs.     Applications should include a curriculum vitae, Research & Leadership Statement, and professional references .     About the Penn State Critical Materials Alliance ( Penn State CMA )   The Penn State CMA i s Penn State's university-wide organization for coordinating research, education, workforce development, industry engagement, and strategic partnerships in critical minerals and materials. Managed by the Materials Research Institute on behalf of the Office of the Senior Vice President for Research, the Alliance brings together faculty, facilities, and programs from across the University to address the entire critical minerals and materials value chain—from resource discovery and extraction to processing, advanced manufacturing, recycling, and supply-chain resilience.   The Penn State CMA serves as Penn State's focal point for interdisciplinary collaboration and external engagement, accelerating large-scale research initiatives, technology translation, workforce development, and partnerships with industry, federal agencies, national laboratories, and state organizations. By integrating expertise across the Colleges of Earth and Mineral Sciences, Engineering, Eberly College of Science, Information Sciences and Technology, Smeal College of Business, the Applied Research Laboratory, and major interdisciplinary institutes—including the Materials Research Institute (MRI), Institute of Energy and the Environment (IEE), National Security Institute (NSI), and Institute for Computational and Data Sciences (ICDS)—the Alliance positions Penn State as a national leader in advancing secure, sustainable, and resilient critical minerals and materials supply chains.     About Penn State   Penn State is a top-ranked public research university and one of the largest and most comprehensive universities in the United States. With more than $1. 4 billion in annual research expenditures and a longstanding commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration, Penn State provides an exceptional environment for research, innovation, education, and public impact.     BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional wellbeing. In addition to comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage, employees enjoy robust retirement plans. One of the standout benefits is the generous 75% tuition discount, available to employees as well as eligible spouses and children.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/job/Penn-State-University-Park/Postdoctoral-Scholar-Position-in-Functional-Ceramic-Materials_REQ_0000080791-1": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar Position in Functional Ceramic Materials",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 25 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Pennsylvania State University invites applications for a Postdoctoral Scholar position in the area of functional ceramic materials in the Randall group. The successful candidate will join an internationally recognized research group conducting cutting-edge research on the processing, characterization, and performance of advanced ceramic materials for energy, electronic, and electromechanical applications within the Materials Research Institute (MRI), The research program focuses on a broad range of functional ceramics, including:  Piezoelectric and ferroelectric materials  Capacitor dielectric materials  Cathode materials for next-generation batteries  Advanced ceramic processing and microstructural engineering  The successful candidate should have demonstrated expertise in the processing, structure, property, and performance relationships of ceramic materials. Experience in one or more of the following areas is highly desirable:  Ceramic powder synthesis and processing  Structural and microstructural characterization  Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM)  Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM)  Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM)  X-ray diffraction (XRD) and related X-ray characterization techniques  Electrical and electromechanical characterization of functional ceramics  Impedance spectroscopy  Electrochemical characterization  Thermally Stimulated Depolarization Current (TSDC) analysis  Ferroelectric, dielectric, piezoelectric, or electrochemical measurements  Other advanced characterization techniques relevant to functional materials  The Postdoctoral Scholar will be expected to:  Conduct independent, high-quality research leading to publications in leading international journals.  Develop and execute innovative research projects aligned with the group's research objectives.  Work collaboratively with graduate students, undergraduate researchers, visiting scientists, and faculty collaborators.  Provide technical guidance and mentorship to students and visiting researchers.  Assist with the operation and development of advanced experimental capabilities within the laboratory.  Contribute to the preparation of research proposals, technical reports, and presentations.  Present research findings at national and international conferences.  Publish research in high-level journals.  Required Qualifications:  Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering, Ceramic Engineering, Physics, Chemistry, or a closely related discipline.  Strong experimental background in functional ceramic materials.  Excellent written and oral communication skills.  Demonstrated publication record in peer-reviewed journals.  Ability to work both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team.  Preferred Qualifications and Experience in one or more of the following areas will be considered an advantage:  Functional oxide ceramics  Ferroelectric and piezoelectric materials  Multilayer ceramic capacitors (MLCCs)  Solid-state electrochemistry  Battery materials  Advanced microscopy and spectroscopy  Reliability and degradation of electronic materials  Data analysis and modeling of structure–property relationships  This position offers the opportunity to work within a highly collaborative and internationally recognized research environment with access to state-of-the-art characterization and processing facilities. The successful candidate will be encouraged to develop independent research directions while contributing to a broad portfolio of sponsored research programs.  Applications should include a cover letter describing research interests and relevant experience, a curriculum vitae, a list of publications, and the names and contact information of three professional references.    BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional well-being.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
